# shir_nodes/gpu_op.node — a GPU device operation lifted from a shell write.
#
# THE §4.2 STAGE ② "gpu-prep" NODE (docs/BASH-O4.md). A shell program draws
# by writing structured text to a device file (the Plan 9 model MIMEcroft's
# fb0 port is built on):
#
#     echo "$blk_p" > /dev/fb0/blocks
#     echo "swap"   > /dev/fb0/call
#
# This node is that write, lifted out of the shell program and into the
# device boundary, so a backend with a GPU leg can emit a dispatch
# (`_sh_gpu_*`, §4.4) instead of a filesystem write. A backend WITHOUT a GPU
# leg renders the node as the original write — the correctness firewall.
#
# Fields:
#   device        the device root ("/dev/fb0", "/dev/webgl")
#   verb          the operation, i.e. the LAST path segment
#                 ("blocks" | "call" | "depthmask" | "hud" | …)
#   slot          the sub-segments before the verb, joined with '/'
#                 (a uniform's "1f/uCamYaw"; empty for a top-level verb)
#   payload       the written value (the batched draw payload, "swap", …)
#   id            a stable per-program id (sh_gpu_dev_<n>), so an emitted
#                 dispatch site and its SPIR-V blob can be named together
#                 (docs/BASH-O4.md §4.4: `_sh_spv_<id>`)

node GpuOp
tag "GpuOp"
field device: string
field verb: string
field slot: string
field payload: expr
field id: string
# RESOLVED — what the node itself fixes is the DEVICE PATH and the LAUNCH GEOMETRY
# of the write it lifts; both are ordinary functions in TextOps#gpu_op_sound:
#  * PATH: path_join builds the written path as the device root, a slash, each slot
#    segment followed by a slash, then the verb — `path_join (device # slot) verb =
#    device @ [47] @ path_join slot verb` (TextOps#gpu_path_last_segment), so the LAST
#    path segment is the verb, and the empty slot is the same statement at slot = [].
#    The verb is TEXT at the tail: appending to it appends to the path
#    (TextOps#gpu_path_verb_is_the_tail), so the device reads exactly the bytes the node
#    was handed. TextOps#gpu_device_path_witness and
#    TextOps#gpu_path_empty_slot_witness check `/dev/fb0` + `call` and the empty-slot
#    `/dev/fb0` + `swap` byte for byte.
#  * GEOMETRY: n work items on blocks of b threads are groups n b = (n + b - 1) div b
#    groups, global thread ids gid b g t = g * b + t, and the work list TextOps#launch.
#    Proved:
#    - the group count COVERS the work, n ≤ groups n b * b (TextOps#groups_cover, from
#      TextOps#groups_decomp), and adds at most a partial group,
#      groups n b * b < n + b (TextOps#groups_tight) — ceil, not floor, and no
#      gratuitous group;
#    - the launch TILES the grid exactly: set (launch_of G b) = {..<G * b}
#      (TextOps#launch_tiles, with TextOps#launch_length, TextOps#launch_bounds and
#      TextOps#distinct_launch_of, whose disjointness comes from blocks being
#      separated by magnitude — TextOps#block_ids_lt — so no modular arithmetic is
#      involved);
#    - consequently every work item i < n is dispatched (TextOps#launch_covers) and
#      the dispatch list is duplicate-free (TextOps#launch_covers_exactly_once).
# NON-VACUITY: TextOps#gpu_launch_witness checks a NON-DIVISIBLE launch (10 items, 4
# threads: 3 groups of 4, the last one half empty) and
# TextOps#gpu_launch_empty_and_single the empty, single-item and one-thread cases.
# Three weaker models are REFUTED: floor rounding misses the tail
# (TextOps#floor_rounding_is_not_a_launch), a block stride of b+1 leaves a hole
# (TextOps#stride_hole_is_not_a_launch), and a launch that covers but repeats a thread
# is not a launch (TextOps#covering_alone_is_not_enough) — coverage alone is not the
# contract.
# NOT PROMISED (outside this model): device memory — buffer allocation and transfers,
# synchronisation, async completion ordering — and the kernel's own effect
# (`out_buffer = kernel(in_buffer)` is the kernel's contract, not the node's). The
# node enqueues work and its own observable value is a completion handle; a backend
# without a GPU leg renders the original write, which is the correctness firewall.
spec: TextOps#gpu_op_sound
